# Shell scripts run on Linux — the pod for build.sh, the container for
# scripts/runpod_start.sh — but are edited on Windows, where core.autocrlf
# rewrites them to CRLF on checkout. A shell script with CRLF fails as
#
#     bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M
#
# which names neither the real cause nor, usually, the right file. Pin them
# to LF everywhere so a checkout, an scp or a `cp -r` into WSL all produce
# something that runs.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same problem, two more file types that are written on Windows and read
# inside a Linux container.
#
# A Dockerfile checked out with CRLF appends a \r to the last argument of
# every command, so `RUN python3 -c "..."` runs and fails on a trailing
# carriage return that nothing in the error message mentions. The
# multi-line RUN blocks in ours make that likelier, not less.
#
# .env.example is copied to .env and read by docker compose, where a \r
# would ride along on the end of the licence key and turn a correct key
# into an invalid one.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf

# The mirror image of the rule above, for the same reason in reverse.
# PowerShell scripts run on Windows and are published to customers, and
# CRLF is what every Windows tool that might open one expects.
#
# What is NOT expressed here, and matters more: both .ps1 files are stored
# with a UTF-8 BOM. powershell.exe (5.1, the default on Windows 11) decodes
# a .ps1 without one as Windows-1252, so the em-dashes in their comments
# each become three characters — one of them a closing quote, which ends a
# string early and makes the file fail to PARSE before it can run a line of
# its own error handling. Git preserves a BOM and has no attribute to
# require one, so `make start-ps1` refuses to publish a start script that
# has lost it.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The generated React bundle. webui_bundle.py is machine-written by
# scripts/gen_webui_bundle.py and committed because neither build host has
# Node — see context.md constraint 5.
#
#   linguist-generated  keeps it out of language statistics and collapses
#                       it in a diff view, where a few hundred KB of
#                       escaped gzip bytes is nothing but noise.
#   -diff               never try to produce a textual diff of it.
#   -merge              and never try to merge one. A conflict here is
#                       ALWAYS resolved by regenerating — take either
#                       side, run `make webui`, commit that. Merging two
#                       halves of a compressed stream produces a file that
#                       still parses as Python and serves a corrupt asset.
webui_bundle.py linguist-generated=true -diff -merge

# LF for the generated module and for everything it is generated from.
# SOURCE_HASH is a hash of those files, so a checkout that turned them
# into CRLF would compute a different hash from the machine that wrote it
# and fail check_webui.py with nothing wrong. (The generator normalises
# CRLF as well — belt and braces, because this rule only takes effect on
# the next checkout of a tree that already has CRLF in it.)
webui_bundle.py text eol=lf
webui/** text eol=lf

# The exceptions to the rule above, and the reason it needs them: `text`
# set explicitly turns OFF git's binary detection, so without these a
# font, an icon or a mask fixture would have its CRLF bytes rewritten on
# checkout and arrive corrupt. There are none tracked under webui/ today;
# these lines are here so that adding the first one is not a bug.
webui/**/*.ico binary
webui/**/*.jpg binary
webui/**/*.png binary
webui/**/*.raw binary
webui/**/*.ttf binary
webui/**/*.webp binary
webui/**/*.woff binary
webui/**/*.woff2 binary
